# What NOT to upload when deploying. Vercel falls back to .gitignore when this
# file is absent, and that fallback is what bit us: the first deploy tried to
# send 23.3MB across more than 5000 files and was refused by the free plan's
# upload limit ("more than 5000, code: api-upload-free"), even though git tracks
# only 126 files totalling well under a megabyte.
#
# The two culprits are build/dev artifacts git ignores but the upload picked up
# anyway — .eve/ alone is 12,775 files. Naming them here removes the ambiguity;
# the source that actually gets deployed is small enough to upload as one archive.

# Dependencies and build output — Vercel installs and builds these itself.
node_modules
.next
out

# eve's LOCAL build cache. The agent is built on Vercel by the service's own
# build command, which WRITES into .eve/vercel-services/… — it is an output
# directory, never an input, so shipping a local copy is pure upload cost.
.eve

# Local-only state and tooling.
.data
.vercel
.git

# Env files. Vercel injects the project's environment variables; a local .env
# would silently shadow them with whatever this machine happens to have.
.env
.env.local
.env*.local
